## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-account access control using IAM roles, Secrets Manager, and KMS in a practical, beginner-friendly setup.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account IAM role and trust policy
- Secrets Manager resource policy and rotation
- CMK policy and private connectivity via VPC endpoints
- Auditing with CloudTrail and Config

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "secretsmanager:GetSecretValue",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:secretsmanager:region:accountS:secret:NAME-*"
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::AccountS:role/LambdaRole"}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify no privilege creep over time?
- What failure modes exist and how would you detect them?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of DynamoDB fine-grained access control, tenant isolation, and rapid breach containment in a realistic multi-tenant SaaS scenario.

## Key Concepts
- DynamoDB LeadingKeys for per-tenant access control
- IAM policies with condition keys to enforce least privilege
- SSE-KMS for data-at-rest protection
- CloudTrail data events for DynamoDB auditing
- Rapid containment via revocation of tokens and tenant quarantine

## Code Example
```json
// IAM policy snippet (conceptual)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["dynamodb:GetItem", "dynamodb.PutItem"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:dynamodb:region:account-id:table/TenantTable",
      "Condition": {
        "ForAllValues:StringEquals": {
          "dynamodb:LeadingKeys": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant_id}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the 60-second containment workflow end-to-end?
- What are the trade-offs of a single-table design vs separate tables per tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cross-account access with least privilege, IAM trust policies, KMS, and auditing; understands CloudTrail, bucket policies.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account IAM roles and trust policies
- S3 bucket policies and access controls
- KMS key policies and encryption scope
- CloudTrail validation and rotation awareness

## Code Example
```json
{
  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",
  \"Statement\": [
    {
      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",
      \"Principal\": {\"AWS\": \"arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A:role/ServiceRoleA\"},
      \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\"
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",
  \"Statement\": [
    {
      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",
      \"Principal\": {\"AWS\": \"arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A:role/ServiceRoleA\"},
      \"Action\": \"s3:GetObject\",
      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-b/data/*\"
    },
    {
      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",
      \"Principal\": {\"AWS\": \"arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A:role/ServiceRoleA\"},
      \"Action\": \"s3:ListBucket\",
      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-b\"
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",
  \"Statement\": [
    {
      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",
      \"Principal\": {\"AWS\": \"arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/DataReadRole\"},
      \"Action\": [\"kms:Decrypt\",\"kms:Encrypt\",\"kms:ReEncrypt*\",\"kms:GenerateDataKey*\"],
      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:kms:REGION:ACCOUNT_B:key/KEYID\"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle key rotation frequency and auditing across accounts?
- How would you test access isolation and ensure no other principals can assume the role?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design scalable, secure cross-account access with ephemeral credentials, tenant isolation, and proactive breach testing in a real-world multi-account data pipeline.

## Key Concepts

- IAM Roles Anywhere + OIDC for short-lived, tenant-scoped credentials
- Lake Formation per-tenant access controls
- Cross-account trust with least privilege
- CMK rotation and key policy governance
- Automated breach simulations, revocation, comprehensive auditing

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "kinesis:DescribeStream",
        "kinesis:GetRecords",
        "kinesis:GetShardIterator"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kinesis:account-a:region:stream/tenant-*-stream",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalTag/TenantID": "${aws:username}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical, implementable security controls for multi-tenant data on AWS, focusing on least-privilege access, data at rest encryption, and rapid breach containment.

## Key Concepts

- STS AssumeRole with tenant-scoped attributes
- S3 bucket policy and per-tenant prefixes for isolation
- KMS per-tenant CMKs with rotation
- CloudTrail data events for auditability
- 60-second containment workflow to revoke credentials and quarantine tenant data

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-bucket",
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-bucket/tenant-${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant}/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "s3:prefix": "tenant/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the 60-second containment workflow in a live environment?
- Which logging and alerting signals validate immutable audit trails during containment?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement tenant isolation with least privilege, plus rapid containment using AWS-native controls. It also covers S3 prefix scoping, per-tenant KMS keys, CloudTrail data events, and automated revocation.

## Key Concepts
- Least privilege IAM for Lambda and prefix-scoped S3 access
- S3 bucket policies with bucket-level and object-prefix constraints
- Per-tenant KMS keys and rotation
- CloudTrail Data Events for S3 access
- EventBridge-based containment workflow (60s)

## Code Example
```javascript
// IAM policy snippet for Lambda role
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake",
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/tenant-id-${aws:PrincipalTag tenantId}/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringLike": {"s3:prefix": "tenant-id-*"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle role cred leakage or bucket misconfiguration?
- How would you test the containment workflow end-to-end?
